WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.260
Wait…did Otto Hightower really&nbsp;
just say that? And why would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.160
he even suggest such a thing? Here's&nbsp;
the creepiest thing that's happened in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.660
House of the Dragon so far. Beware:&nbsp;
spoilers for episode 3, just ahead!

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.280
It is known that House of the Dragon and Game of&nbsp;
Thrones have been partly inspired by real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.140
historical events, while at the same time being&nbsp;
filled to the brim with creepy and awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.000
situations. We suppose that is to be expected,&nbsp;
though, since real-life history has its fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.820
share of bad decisions. As reported by BBC, author&nbsp;
George R.R. Martin drew heavily from the real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.620
14th-century conflict known as the "Hundred&nbsp;
Years' War" in the crafting of his novels.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.600
Knowing that series creator Martin is a history&nbsp;
buff, and with fans expecting a certain amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.400
of scheming, viewers are probably watching the&nbsp;
actions of characters very closely. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.140
there are no obvious Littlefinger or Tywin&nbsp;
Lannister types yet, certain actions from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.060
Otto Hightower are starting to raise some red&nbsp;
flags. So far, we have seen Otto manipulate his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.580
own young daughter into marrying King Viserys,&nbsp;
as well as antagonize Daemon Targaryen. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.200
seems as if Otto's recent suggestion in Episode&nbsp;
3 may be the creepiest power grab of all time.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.420
Episode 3 of House of the Dragon is primarily&nbsp;
focused on lines of succession. Previously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.980
Princess Rhaenyra Targaryen is named heir to&nbsp;
the Seven Kingdoms by her father, King Viserys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:21.420
after the death of his newborn son and wife.&nbsp;
Although there has never been a queen in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.800
history of Westeros, Viserys is determined&nbsp;
that he made the right choice. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.220
many of his subjects and advisors&nbsp;
openly question his decision.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.480
This issue of succession is compounded&nbsp;
even more when Viserys marries Alicent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:38.820
and she gives birth to a male heir. Named Aegon,&nbsp;
which apparently is an exceptionally popular name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.900
among the Targaryens, the child is assumed&nbsp;
by the realm to be the eventual new heir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.400
so much so that other royal houses make&nbsp;
marriage proposals aimed at Rhaenyra. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.660
most insufferable proposal fans have seen&nbsp;
thus far has been that of Jason Lannister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.200
who has all of the trademark arrogance&nbsp;
of others of the same last name.

00:01:58.059 --> 00:01:59.199
"Thank you for the wine."

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.940
Though Rhaenyra rebuffs Jason's advances,&nbsp;
and even fights with her father over his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.660
attempts to find her a suitable match, she&nbsp;
declares that she doesn't want to marry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:14.820
and just wants to be queen. King Viserys then&nbsp;
proclaims that as royalty, they are honor-bound to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.380
tradition and that they must marry to strengthen&nbsp;
bloodlines and the lines of succession. He then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.520
adds that he will not force her hand and that&nbsp;
she can marry whomever she chooses. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:29.340
that isn't an idea Otto Hightower thinks is good,&nbsp;
and he has an entirely different suggestion.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.240
Otto suggests that Viserys&nbsp;
betroth Rhaenyra to Aegon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.440
which should immediately get viewers to recoil&nbsp;
in disgust as Otto honestly considers having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.700
a half-sister and brother marry each other.&nbsp;
This isn't unheard of in Targaryen history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.920
as they have a reputation for marrying family&nbsp;
members for the so-called preservation of a "pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.720
bloodline." This has been previously mentioned&nbsp;
by Cersei and Jamie Lannister in Game of Thrones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.640
who justify their own relationship by referencing&nbsp;
the Targaryen tradition of marrying siblings.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.160
Considering the political&nbsp;
maneuvering of Otto Hightower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:04.140
this makes sense as he wants to strengthen his&nbsp;
own grandson's claim on the Iron Throne. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.620
there is some debate as to who the heir&nbsp;
will be, by marrying the two siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.760
there would be one unified claim. This&nbsp;
isn't without historical precedent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.900
as real-life royal marriage between blood&nbsp;
relatives was relatively common up until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:21.660
onset of World War I. This practice, perceived as&nbsp;
keeping royal blood pure, often resulted in rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.520
genetic diseases and physical abnormalities&nbsp;
– and was also, of course, insanely gross.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.680
As previously mentioned, the Targaryens do have a&nbsp;
habit of marrying blood relatives, and the realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.540
often suffers for it. Whatever his reasons, Otto&nbsp;
should not be absolved for making the suggestion;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.680
the thought of it is stomach churning. Although&nbsp;
he seems to have his sights on asserting control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.352
of the Iron Throne, he can also relish in his&nbsp;
newly acquired award for "creepiest advice ever."

